===Etymology===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = A Tanzanite gemcutter brings out the sparkle in the stone.jpg
|caption1 = A [[Tanzanian#Adjective|Tanzanian]] lapidary ''(noun {{senseno|en|person}})'' [[work#Verb|working]] on [[tanzanite]].
|image2 = Fotothek df tg 0004965 Bergbau ^ Geologie ^ Gestein ^ Schmuck ^ Ring.jpg
|caption2 = The [[title page]] of a 1565 lapidary ''(noun {{senseno|en|treatise}})'' by the [[Swiss#Adjective|Swiss]] [[naturalist]] and [[physician]] {{w|Conrad Gessner}}.
}}

{{root|en|ine-pro|*lep-}}
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|lapidari}}, {{m|enm|lapidarie|t=person who cuts, polishes, or engraves precious stones; expert in precious stones; treatise on precious stones}}{{nb...|lapadari, lapadary, lapidaire, lapidarye, lipidarye, (Scotland) lapidar|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=lapidārī(e|pos=n|id=MED24660}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|lapidaire|t=gemsmith, lapidary}} (modern {{cog|fr|lapidaire}}), or from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|lapidārius|t=(''adjective'') of stones, stony; (''noun'') stonecutter}}, from {{m|la|lapidis}} (the {{glossary|genitive}} {{glossary|singular}} of {{m|la|lapis|t=stone; (''poetic'') jewel, precious stone}}, possibly from {{der|en|qsb-grc|-}} or {{der|en|ine-pro|*lep-|t=to peel}})<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=2123467960|date=September 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj.'' and ''n}}</ref> + {{m|la|-ārius|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming adjectives}}.

{{senseno|en|jewellery|uc=1}} (“jewellery”) and {{senseno|en|treatise}} (“treatise on precious stones”) are derived from {{der|en|la|lapidāria}} or {{m|la|lapidārium}}, a noun use of the {{glossary|neuter}} {{glossary|plural}} or genitive plural respectively of {{m|la|lapidāris|t=of stone|pos=adjective}}, from {{m|la|lapidis}} (the genitive singular of {{m|la|lapis}}; see above) + {{m|la|-āris|pos=suffix forming adjectives}}.<ref name="OED"/>

The {{glossary|adjective}} is either:

* a {{lbor|en|la|lapidārius|pos=adjective|nocap=1}};<ref name="OED"/> or
* derived from the noun.

