===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Of or pertaining to [[gem#Noun|gems]] and [[precious stone]]s, or the [[art#Noun|art]] of [[work#Verb|working]] them.
# {{non-gloss definition|Senses relating to [[inscription]]s.}}
## Of an inscription: [[engrave]]d on [[stone#Noun|stone]].
##* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|quotee=Johnson|year=1775|page=514|passage=The vvriter of an epitaph ſhould not be conſidered as ſaying nothing but vvhat is ſtrictly true. Allovvance muſt be made for ſome degree of exaggerated praiſe. In '''lapidary''' inſcriptions a man is not upon oath.}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Vision of Judgment|stanza=XII|page=7|passage=He's dead—and upper earth with him has done: / He's buried; save the undertaker's bill, / Or '''lapidary''' scrawl, the world is gone / For him, {{...}}}}
## Of a [[piece#Noun|piece]] of [[writing#Noun|writing]] or a writing [[style#Noun|style]]: [[characteristic#Adjective|characteristic]] of or [[suitable]] for an inscription; [[embody]]ing the [[precision#Noun|precision]] and [[refinement]] of inscriptions on [[monument#Noun|monuments]]; [[concise#Adjective|concise]] and [[stately]].
##: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:concise}}
##: {{antonyms|en|Thesaurus:verbose}}
##* {{RQ:Carlyle Sartor Resartus|chapter=Getting Under Way|page=91|passage=His grand principle is, that '''lapidary''' inscriptions, of what sort soever, should be Historical rather than Lyrical.}}
##* {{RQ:Hallam Literature of Europe|volume=II|chapter=History of Physical and Miscellaneous Literature from 1500 to 1600|section=VII (General State of Literature)|para=64|page=503|passage=They were the encouragers of a numismatic and '''lapidary''' erudition, elegant in itself, and throwing for ever its little sparks of light on the still ocean of the past, but not very favourable to comprehensive observation, and tending to bestow on an unprofitable pedantry the honours of real learning.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Karen Armstrong|chapter=Christians: Brave New World (1492–1870)|title=The Battle for God|series=A [[w:Ballantine Books|Ballantine Book]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Random House|The Random House Publishing Group]]|year=2000|year_published=February 2001|section=part 1 (The Old World and the New)|page=71|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=25jHvH33vZgC&pg=PA71|isbn=978-0-345-39169-8|passage=A man of the new age, he &#91;{{w|René Descartes}}&#93; would not accept received ideas; the scientist must make his mind a ''tabula rasa''. The sole truth was that supplied by mathematics or by such '''lapidary''' propositions as "What's done cannot be undone," which was irrefutably correct.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|rare}} Of or pertaining to stones [[in general]].
#* {{RQ:De Quincey Works|volume=VI|page=164|passage=Yet such is the temper of this world, that, if a grave philosopher, by shaking his fist, and other acts of bravado, should happen to provoke a company of mischievous boys to reply with a shower of stones, people in general suffer their resentment to settle upon the philosopher for his wanton provocation, rather than on the boys for that '''lapidary''' style of retort in which their wrath has been trained to express itself.}}

